From c92316fad9a0d7e8ae4e76502db94e706555b3b0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 5 Aug 2026 14:36:28 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Add ticker-search-based alias widening, a generalized fix
 beyond suffix-stripping

Re-running the fixed Sonata Software case surfaced real coverage where
there had been none - but the CEO-transition story from the same window
still didn't come through. Press headlines about leadership changes often
drop every word but the company's leading one: "Sonata appoints Rajsekhar
Datta Roy as CEO," not "Sonata Software appoints...". Verified directly:
that headline scores 0.216 against the current aliases, still below the
0.35 threshold.

Adding the leading word unconditionally would reopen the conglomerate-
sibling bug this project already found and fixed once (a bare "Tata" alias
matching Tata Motors/Steel/Power articles meant for a different Tata Group
company). A per-company alias list isn't a real fix either - it just moves
the same silent gap to the next company nobody thought to check.

ceia/ingest.py:widen_aliases() asks the question a human fact-checker
would instead: search for the bare leading word via the same ticker-search
endpoint --ticker auto-detection already uses, and only trust it if this
company is the top result. "Sonata" resolves to Sonata Software and gets
added; "Tata" resolves to whichever Tata Group company Yahoo ranks
highest, essentially never the specific sibling a given run is actually
about, so it's correctly left out - with no hardcoded list of "risky"
conglomerate names to write or maintain.

Wired into ingest.run() (used for both the slug pre-filter and relevance
scoring), reported via a new IngestResult.aliases_used field so the run's
own JSON always states what was actually searched for, and gated by a new
--skip-alias-widening flag on both ingest.py and analyze.py's CLIs.
Best-effort like ticker auto-detection: any lookup failure degrades to
skipping the extra alias rather than failing the run.

Adds tests/test_ingest.py::TestWidenAliases (7 tests, driven through a
fake session the same way test_ticker_lookup.py already does) covering the
safe case, the conglomerate-sibling refusal, lookup-failure degradation,
no-equity-results degradation, no-duplicate-alias, cross-exchange-listing
matching, and a company name with no usable leading word. 385 tests
passing, up from 378.
